1. Minikube & Kubernetes Commands

Scenario: “How would you troubleshoot a Pod that is not starting?”

“First, I would check the Pod status using kubectl get pods. Then I would use kubectl describe pod to check events and identify issues such as image pull errors, 
insufficient resources, or scheduling problems. I would also check the container logs using kubectl logs. Based on the error, I would fix the underlying issue and 
verify the Pod becomes Ready.”

2. K8s Architecture, Labels, Selectors & Node Selectors

Scenario: “You have a workload that must run only on specific nodes. What would you do?”

“I would label the required nodes using kubectl label nodes. Then I would use nodeSelector or node affinity in the Deployment specification. This ensures Kubernetes 
schedules those Pods only on the appropriate nodes. For more flexible scheduling requirements, I would prefer node affinity.”

3. Kubernetes Services

Scenario: “Pods are running, but users cannot access the application. What would you check?”

“I would first verify the Service and its endpoints. I would check whether the Service selector matches the Pod labels. Then I would verify the targetPort and 
containerPort, and test connectivity from another Pod. If the application is externally exposed, I would also check the LoadBalancer, Ingress, or security rules.”

4. ReplicationController & ReplicaSet

Scenario: “A Pod suddenly gets deleted. How does Kubernetes recover it?”

“A ReplicaSet continuously maintains the desired number of Pods. If a Pod is deleted or crashes, the ReplicaSet controller detects that the actual number is below the 
desired state and creates a replacement Pod. In modern Kubernetes, ReplicaSets are generally managed through Deployments.”

5. Deployment & HPA

Scenario: “Your application suddenly receives 5x normal traffic. How would Kubernetes handle it?”

“I would configure an HPA based on CPU, memory, or custom metrics. When utilization crosses the configured threshold, HPA increases the number of Pod replicas. 
The Deployment manages those replicas, while the Service distributes traffic across healthy Pods. I would also ensure the cluster has enough node capacity or configure 
Cluster Autoscaler where applicable.”

6. StatefulSet & DaemonSet

Scenario: “Why would you use StatefulSet instead of Deployment?”

“I would use StatefulSet when the application requires stable identity, persistent storage, or ordered deployment and termination. For example, databases like Cassandra
or Kafka can require stable Pod identities. A Deployment is more suitable for stateless applications where Pods are interchangeable.”

DaemonSet scenario:

“If I need to run a monitoring or logging agent on every Kubernetes node, I would use a DaemonSet. Kubernetes automatically creates one Pod per eligible node and also 
schedules the Pod on newly added nodes.”

7. Namespace & ConfigMap

Scenario: “How would you separate Dev, QA and Production workloads?”

“I would use Kubernetes namespaces to logically isolate resources. For example, dev, qa, and prod namespaces. I can apply different RBAC permissions, resource quotas, 
and configurations to each namespace. Application configuration that isn't sensitive can be stored in ConfigMaps and injected into Pods as environment variables or
mounted files.”

8. Secrets & ResourceQuota

Scenario: “Your application needs database credentials. Where would you store them?”

“I would avoid storing credentials directly in the Deployment YAML or Git repository. I would use Kubernetes Secrets, preferably integrated with an external secrets
manager such as AWS Secrets Manager depending on the architecture. Access would be controlled using RBAC, and I would ensure sensitive values aren't exposed through 
logs or configuration files.”

ResourceQuota scenario:

“If one team is consuming most of the cluster resources, I would configure a ResourceQuota at the namespace level. This limits the total CPU, memory, Pods, or other
resources that namespace can consume and prevents one team from impacting other workloads.”

9. Secrets & ResourceQuota

Scenario: “A team deploys too many Pods and impacts other teams. What would you do?”

“I would first identify the resource consumption using Kubernetes metrics. Then I would configure namespace-level ResourceQuota and LimitRange policies. This 
controls the maximum resources a namespace can consume and can also enforce default CPU and memory requests and limits.”

10. Argo CD / GitOps

Scenario: “A developer manually changes a Kubernetes Deployment in production. What happens in a GitOps setup?”

“With Argo CD, Git is treated as the source of truth. If someone manually changes the Kubernetes resource, Argo CD can detect the drift between the desired state 
in Git and the live cluster. Depending on the sync configuration, it can either alert us or automatically reconcile the cluster back to the Git-defined state.”

Interview line:
“Git → Argo CD → Kubernetes”

11. RBAC

Scenario: “A developer needs access to Pods but should not be able to delete Deployments. How would you implement it?”

“I would use Kubernetes RBAC. I would create a Role with only the required permissions, such as get, list, and watch on Pods. Then I would create a RoleBinding to 
associate that Role with the developer or service account. I follow the principle of least privilege rather than giving broad permissions.”

12. Kubernetes Volumes

Scenario: “A Pod is deleted and the application loses its data. How would you prevent this?”

“For persistent data, I would use PersistentVolumes and PersistentVolumeClaims instead of relying on the Pod's ephemeral filesystem. In AWS EKS, depending on the workload,
I could use EBS or EFS through the appropriate CSI driver. The PVC allows the workload to request persistent storage independently of the Pod lifecycle.”

13. Helm

Scenario: “You have the same application deployed in Dev, QA and Production with different configurations. How would Helm help?”

“I would create a Helm chart containing reusable Kubernetes templates and maintain environment-specific values files. For example, values-dev.yaml, values-qa.yaml,
and values-prod.yaml. This avoids duplicating Kubernetes manifests and makes deployments consistent across environments.”

14. Network Policies

Scenario: “Your frontend Pods should communicate with backend Pods, but backend Pods should not be directly accessible from other namespaces. How would you implement it?”

“I would use Kubernetes NetworkPolicies to control Pod-to-Pod traffic. I would allow traffic to the backend only from the required frontend Pods or namespace and deny 
unnecessary ingress traffic. I would also verify that the Kubernetes CNI supports NetworkPolicy enforcement.”

15. Probes & Ingress

Scenario: “The application is running, but sometimes receives traffic before it is actually ready. How would you solve it?”

“I would configure a readiness probe. Kubernetes only sends Service traffic to Pods that pass the readiness check. I would also use a liveness probe to detect applications
that are stuck and need restarting. For slow-starting applications, I may also configure a startup probe to prevent premature liveness failures.”

Ingress scenario:

“If multiple applications need to be exposed through a common HTTP/HTTPS entry point, I would use an Ingress with an Ingress Controller. For example, /api can route to 
the backend Service and / to the frontend Service. TLS termination can also be configured at the Ingress layer.”

16. Deployment Strategies & Jobs

Scenario: “You need to release a high-risk application version with minimal production risk. What would you choose?”

“For a high-risk release, I would consider Canary or Blue-Green deployment. With Canary, I can expose the new version to a small percentage of traffic, monitor metrics 
and gradually increase traffic. With Blue-Green, I can keep the previous version available and quickly switch traffic back if the new version has issues.”

Job scenario:

“If I need to perform a one-time database backup or migration, I would use a Kubernetes Job. If the task needs to run periodically, such as a daily backup, I would use 
a CronJob.”
